00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:07.416
Starships refusal to fly in Apollo style one-shot moon trip is the engineering angle behind

00:05:07.416 --> 00:05:08.776
those lander interiors.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:15.530
Apollo's Saturn V put roughly 47 tons on a trans lunar path by discarding stages after

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:16.070
each burn.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:22.170
Starship must return its entire 100-plus tonne upper stage and land that ship intact.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:27.210
Keep that much hardware through every firing and you are accelerating a vehicle on the order

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.910
of a loaded jetliner, not a tiny capsule.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.310
The rocket equation is exponential.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:37.630
The fueled mass over the empty mass equals e to the power of delta v divided by exhaust

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.310
velocity.

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:44.030
Vacuum methane engines deliver an exhaust velocity on the order of 3.5 kilometers per

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:44.390
second.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:49.830
Translunar injection, the burn out of Earth orbit, is roughly 3 kilometers per second,

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:55.530
so that burn alone wants a mass ratio near 2.5 before you even count landing or ascent.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:00.830
Throw the tanks away, as Saturn did, and each later burn only has to push what remains.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.770
Keep the ship and the propellant to land, leave, and still have a vehicle worth flying

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:06.190
again.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.550
We'll not fit in a stack you can lift from Earth in one go.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.510
That is why tankers exist.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:15.710
They aggregate propellant in low Earth orbit, then the crude lander leaves.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:21.810
NASA's updated Artemis III plan now does that aggregation before Orion launches, and

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:26.730
It has the fueled starship light the moon-bound burn from high-earth orbit rather than a rendez-vous

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:27.630
in lunar orbit.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:32.530
Doing the moon-burn from high-earth orbit means some of the hard work of leaving already

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.210
happened after the tanks were full.

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:39.570
You spend extra launches, you get a lander you can fly again, and a habitat Apollo never

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:40.970
had the mass to leave behind.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.790
Early estimates of 22 tanker flights have been cut to 6-8 on better transfer efficiency

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:47.250
targets.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.150
In microgravity, liquid does not sit on the outlet unless you settle it with a small

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:57.970
burn, and every hour in sunlight warms cryogenic oxygen and methane until some of it vents as

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:58.550
boil-off.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.910
The original plan assumed you would dump a lot of each load.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:06.090
The new target assumes most of each tanker actually arrives in the receiver.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:12.510
The first on-orbit demonstration is expected in early 2027, measuring those boil-off and

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.090
transfer losses in flight rather than on a stand.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:20.590
Once that is validated, the same tanker fleet supports lunar landings and later Mars cargo

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.470
without redesigning the core vehicle.

